WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.034
Picture a power cut on the café's street, two seconds after a customer taps their card.

2
00:00:08.034 --> 00:00:10.180
The card reader had already beeped.

3
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:15.055
The payments program had written the payment to its log file, and said approved.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.615
When the power comes back, the payment isn't in the file.

5
00:00:18.615 --> 00:00:20.412
Every step had said it worked.

6
00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:22.089
So where did the payment go?

7
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:23.826
Let's watch a write.

8
00:00:23.826 --> 00:00:27.866
A program adds a record to a file with a call named write.

9
00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:30.304
It returns success in microseconds.

10
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:34.826
And any program that reads the file straight after sees the new record.

11
00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:37.221
So far, it looks like it's on the disk.

12
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.737
Here's a bigger test from the lab.

13
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:45.031
A command called dd writes 64 megabytes to a file, in a fraction of a second.

14
00:00:45.031 --> 00:00:51.905
Right after, the kernel's own count says: 64 megabytes of that file are in RAM, and not on the disk.

15
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.158
So when does it reach the disk?

16
00:00:54.158 --> 00:00:55.237
Pause and guess.

17
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.170
About 30 seconds later, when nothing else was going on.

18
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.105
Until then, the newest data was only in RAM.

19
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.432
Here's what write really does.

20
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:12.647
It copies your bytes into the page cache, the kernel's copy of file pages in RAM.

21
00:01:12.647 --> 00:01:16.124
It marks each page dirty, meaning newer than the disk.

22
00:01:16.124 --> 00:01:17.089
And it returns.

23
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.343
Why not wait for the disk each time?

24
00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:22.795
Then every small write would pay for a trip to the device.

25
00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:27.140
And a page rewritten a hundred times in a second can go to the disk once.

26
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.581
A post office works the same way.

27
00:01:29.581 --> 00:01:31.347
You hand the clerk a letter.

28
00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:34.815
The clerk says done, and drops it in the outgoing tray.

29
00:01:34.815 --> 00:01:37.148
Now and then, a van empties the tray.

30
00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:42.308
And anyone who asks for your letter before the van comes gets it straight from the tray.

31
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.105
So the clerk wasn't lying.

32
00:01:44.105 --> 00:01:46.500
Your letter really is in the building.

33
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.012
It just hasn't left yet.

34
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.129
Let's map it.

35
00:01:49.129 --> 00:01:51.848
The clerk saying done is write returning.

36
00:01:51.848 --> 00:01:53.639
The tray is the page cache.

37
00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:56.093
A letter in the tray is a dirty page.

38
00:01:56.093 --> 00:02:03.191
And the van is called writeback: kernel threads, called flushers, that write dirty pages to the disk later.

39
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.251
Four things send the van.

40
00:02:05.251 --> 00:02:06.022
First, age.

41
00:02:06.022 --> 00:02:13.376
A page that has been dirty for 30 seconds goes at the flusher's next wake-up, every 5 seconds by default.

42
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.802
Second, a full tray.

43
00:02:14.802 --> 00:02:20.468
When dirty data passes 10 percent of the memory it could use, the flusher starts early.

44
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.988
Third, an overflowing tray.

45
00:02:22.988 --> 00:02:27.623
Past 20 percent, a program that writes more is made to wait, and help.

46
00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:30.073
That's when write suddenly gets slow.

47
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:34.101
And fourth, the program asks, with a call named fsync.

48
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.301
Let's follow the log file.

49
00:02:36.301 --> 00:02:37.825
Record 1 is a payment.

50
00:02:37.825 --> 00:02:41.773
write copies it into page 0, marks it dirty, and returns.

51
00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:45.236
If the power went out now, record 1 would be lost.

52
00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:47.740
30 seconds later, the flusher wakes.

53
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.081
Page 0 has been dirty long enough, so it goes to the disk, and the page is clean.

54
00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:56.672
Record 2 is a refund the program must not lose.

55
00:02:56.672 --> 00:02:58.628
write returns just as fast.

56
00:02:58.628 --> 00:03:02.396
So before it says approved, the program calls fsync.

57
00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:08.813
The kernel writes the page, tells the disk to save it, and returns only when the disk confirms.

58
00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:12.692
Milliseconds instead of microseconds, and record 2 is safe.

59
00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:15.277
Record 3 is written, and not forced.

60
00:03:15.277 --> 00:03:17.177
It sits in the cache, dirty.

61
00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.693
Now, two different accidents.

62
00:03:19.693 --> 00:03:23.047
In one, the program crashes, killed on the spot.

63
00:03:23.047 --> 00:03:25.353
In the other, the power goes out.

64
00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:27.099
Which one loses record 3?

65
00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:28.217
Pause and guess.

66
00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:32.679
Only the power cut.

67
00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:36.218
The dirty pages belong to the kernel, not the program.

68
00:03:36.218 --> 00:03:40.413
So after a crash, the flusher still writes record 3 to the disk.

69
00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:45.949
A power cut wipes RAM, and record 3 goes with it, although its write said success.

70
00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:49.028
Here's where the post office picture breaks.

71
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.593
A tray of letters survives a power cut.

72
00:03:51.593 --> 00:03:52.383
RAM doesn't.

73
00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:55.225
So is half a minute in RAM a problem?

74
00:03:55.225 --> 00:03:56.488
For most files, no.

75
00:03:56.488 --> 00:03:59.279
For a payment that was just approved, yes.

76
00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:02.735
If you'll tell someone it's saved, call fsync first.

77
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:08.325
It costs milliseconds each time, and the next part of the lesson shows how to share that cost.

78
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.012
And the café's payment?

79
00:04:10.012 --> 00:04:14.550
It was approved two seconds before the power cut, still dirty in RAM.

80
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.035
One fsync before saying approved would have saved it.

81
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.087
So the moral of the story: write returns when the kernel has your bytes.

82
00:04:23.087 --> 00:04:25.496
fsync returns when the disk has them.

83
00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:26.796
One quick check.

84
00:04:26.796 --> 00:04:31.739
The program writes record 4 into page 0, and is killed a second later.

85
00:04:31.739 --> 00:04:33.081
The power stays on.

86
00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:35.834
Is record 4 on the disk a minute later?

87
00:04:35.834 --> 00:04:36.964
Pause and think.

88
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:40.469
Yes.

89
00:04:40.469 --> 00:04:47.061
The kernel still holds the dirty page, and the flusher writes it about 30 seconds after the write.
